Yes. And I’d separate “I’m bad at people” from the mechanisms underneath it.

What you describe could come from a lot of places: years of operating in a very unusual culture, having a twin who provided built-in companionship, being out of practice, stress/burnout, attention being spread too thin, or simply having learned relationships differently from most people. Autism or neurological injury are also things a clinician could evaluate, but nothing you’ve said by itself establishes either one. If this represents a major change from how you used to be—especially alongside memory, impulse-control, headaches, language, or personality changes—a neuropsychological evaluation would be reasonable. If it has been lifelong, an adult autism assessment could answer a different question.

But you don’t need to solve the diagnosis first. Social connection is trainable.

The biggest adjustment I’d make is this: stop treating conversations primarily as information transfer. A lot of bonding is really mutual regulation + attention + small disclosures over repeated encounters.

Military/task communication often looks like:

problem → relevant information → decision → action.

Friendly civilian communication has an extra layer:

notice person → signal warmth → exchange something small → respond emotionally → then maybe solve something.

Someone says, “My daughter started kindergarten today.”

Task brain hears: information received.

Connection brain hears: This person is giving me a little doorway into his life.

So instead of “Nice,” you might say, “Damn, how’d she do? Were you more nervous than she was?”

That extra five seconds is where a surprising amount of friendship happens.

I’d train five things deliberately:

* Warmth before competence. Eye contact, slight smile, relaxed face, use their name, greet people first. You don’t have to become bubbly. Just make it easy to tell that you’re glad they’re there.
* Curiosity without interrogation. Ask one question, listen to the answer, then offer something about yourself. Question → answer → related disclosure. That keeps it from becoming a debrief.
* Emotional acknowledgment before solutions. “That sounds brutal.” “That’s awesome.” “I’d be pissed too.” Then, if appropriate: “Want ideas, or are you just venting?”
* Small vulnerability. Friendship generally requires giving people information they didn’t strictly need to know: what you’re excited about, what confused you, something funny that happened, something you’re trying to improve. Not trauma-dumping—just becoming knowable.
* Consistency. Most friendships aren’t forged by one brilliant conversation. They’re accumulated. Send the text. Remember the birthday. Ask how the thing went. Invite them again even if the first invitation didn’t work.

And I think the last one may matter especially for you. If you grew up with an identical twin, you may have gotten an unusually large amount of relationship continuity for free. You didn’t need to consciously construct companionship from scratch every few months. Most friendships require maintenance behaviors that can feel bizarrely artificial if you never had to practice them.

You can systematize that without making friendship transactional. Keep perhaps 5–10 people in your active orbit. Once a week, look at the names and ask: Who haven’t I talked to? Who has something going on? Who should I invite somewhere?

Then send something tiny:

“Hey man, thought about you. How’d that interview go?”

“I’m taking the kids to ___ Saturday if you want to come.”

“Saw this and immediately thought of you.”

That is friendship maintenance. It doesn’t need to be profound.

For meeting new people, I would strongly favor recurring environments over random networking events. A weekly class, fishing group, lifting gym, church/community activity, volunteering, golf league, kids’ activities, veterans organization, whatever genuinely interests you. Familiarity does enormous work. Seeing the same person fifteen times removes the pressure to establish instant chemistry.

Networking works similarly. Don’t enter thinking, What can this person do for me? Nor the opposite, How can I impress them?

Try:

Who is this person? What are they building? What do they care about? Is there any useful connection I can make?

If there’s one thing you can give—an introduction, useful information, a recommendation, a laugh—do it without immediately demanding reciprocity. Over time you become someone who is useful and pleasant to have around, which is a much stronger network position than simply being impressive.

There’s also a useful conversion for your operator instincts:

Don’t suppress them. Translate them.

Situational awareness becomes noticing who’s being left out.

Leadership becomes introducing two people who should know one another.

Debriefing becomes remembering what someone told you last week.

Loyalty becomes showing up when somebody’s having a shitty month.

Courage becomes saying, “Hey, I’ve been bad about staying in touch. Want to grab lunch?”

Protectiveness becomes kindness without infantilizing anyone.

Precision becomes learning what actually makes a particular person feel cared about instead of assuming everybody wants what you would want.

That’s still you. It’s just the prosocial expression of the same machinery.

And one deceptively powerful practice: every day, create three tiny positive interactions with strangers or acquaintances. Tell the cashier something sincerely nice. Ask another dad about his kid. Joke with the guy beside you. Say hello first at the gym. No objective beyond making the interaction 5% warmer than it would otherwise have been.

You’re basically doing social Zone 2: low stakes, lots of volume, no need to perform.

Over several months, I’d judge progress less by “Do people like me?” and more by:

Do people know me? Do I know them? Do I initiate? Do I follow up? Do I notice what matters to them? Can I disagree without withdrawing or dominating? Can I be useful without needing to be needed? Can I let somebody else matter for a while?

Those are much closer to the actual architecture of being a good friend, husband, father, teammate, or network connection.

And importantly, kind does not mean compliant. A socially skilled person can be warm and still say no, disagree, enforce a boundary, or leave a bad relationship. The target isn’t becoming softer everywhere. It’s developing more gears.